WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.737
Nitrogen does not burn, but it still plays a big part in every fire.

2
00:00:05.178 --> 00:00:11.819
In Earth's air, every oxygen molecule comes with almost four molecules of nitrogen.

3
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:19.354
A flame has to heat all of that nitrogen too. It acts like a sponge, soaking up heat.

4
00:00:19.865 --> 00:00:24.521
Raise the oxygen percent, and there is less nitrogen to share the heat.

5
00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:33.867
In a Moon Base atmosphere of thirty percent oxygen, each oxygen molecule brings only about two and a third nitrogen molecules.

6
00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:39.544
In a spacesuit, filled with pure oxygen, there is none at all.

7
00:00:40.044 --> 00:00:46.487
With less nitrogen to warm, the flame burns hotter, spreads faster, and is harder to put out.

8
00:00:46.986 --> 00:00:51.166
This is why the same breathing does not mean the same fire risk.

9
00:00:51.851 --> 00:01:01.394
It also explains how some extinguishers work. Carbon dioxide or helium dilutes the oxygen and carries heat away from the flame.

10
00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:09.196
NASA's FLEX experiment tested exactly that, on burning droplets aboard the space station.
